reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
There is real inference at every choice:
- the binomial over the opponents' unseen dice, conditioned on your own hand;
- reading the opener's face choice as evidence of what it holds;
- weighing Dudo against Calza against raising onto your own face to pass the risk on;
- planning the forced final call.

A probability-aware player beats the thoughtless policies above by wide margins. For example, Dudo on a minimum opening wins about .13 with j=1, whereas raising on 3 held matches is true .868. Each round is decided under partial information, so a single round is fairly noisy, perhaps 40-50% chance. A game spans about 9 or more rounds in 2 players and 15 or more in 4 players, which concentrates the skill edge. I estimate the stronger reasoner wins roughly 65-75% against a weak one.

When both players follow good strategy, the outcome still turns on bluff and inference choices (which face to open, when to raise falsely), not on the deal alone. The dice are a fresh deal each round that good play can overcome, not a fixed hidden type that settles the game.
